{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",
  // Repo-local policy only. Shared org/GitOps extraction rules live in
  // webgrip/renovate-config; runtime/admin-only settings live in the
  // in-cluster Renovate ConfigMap.
  // Pinned to a semver release tag. webgrip/renovate-config uses semantic-release
  // with protected tags so these are immutable and auto-updatable by Renovate.
  extends: [
    // local> = resolved from the Renovate platform host — the in-cluster Forgejo, where
    // renovate-config is authoritative (Forgejo-leading). github> would read the downstream
    // push-mirror copy instead of the source of truth.
    "local>webgrip/renovate-config#v1.6.0",
    "local>webgrip/renovate-config:gitops#v1.6.0",
    "docker:enableMajor",
    ":automergeBranch",
  ],
  dependencyDashboardTitle: "Renovate Dashboard 🤖",
  // Resolve the Harbor Docker Hub pull-through path against Docker Hub itself. The NOTE further
  // down used to say registryAliases "keys match on registry host only, so they can't
  // disambiguate multiple upstreams behind one Harbor host+path". That is wrong — Renovate
  // matches `currentFrom.startsWith(name + "/")`, a PATH PREFIX — and the alias then does exactly
  // the split we want:
  //     packageName -> docker.io/<repo>          (what Renovate looks up)
  //     depName     -> harbor.webgrip.dev/...    (what stays written in the manifest)
  // So version discovery and digest resolution go to Docker Hub while every pull still goes
  // through Harbor. The shared preset already uses this exact pattern for mirror.gcr.io.
  //
  // WHY IT MATTERS — two bugs, one fix:
  //  1. Renovate only reads release timestamps from Docker Hub's `tag_last_pushed` field (its own
  //     docker datasource says "Only supported on Docker Hub"). A harbor.* packageName therefore
  //     has NO timestamp, which means minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour: timestamp-optional treats the
  //     soak as satisfied. Every Harbor-proxied dep has effectively been merging with a ZERO-day
  //     soak, and the `Released` PR column could only ever print an em dash for them.
  //  2. Harbor's proxy intermittently fails digest resolution — the dashboard has been carrying
  //     "Could not determine new digest for update" for vikunja and both sparkyfitness images.
  //     Resolving against Docker Hub avoids the proxy for lookups entirely.
  //
  // NOT aliasing the ghcr/quay/forgejo Harbor projects: those upstreams report no release
  // timestamps either, so it would add blast radius for zero gain.
  //
  // COVERAGE IS PARTIAL — registryAliases supportedManagers covers kubernetes/kustomize/helmfile/
  // dockerfile but NOT helm-values, so ~8 HelmRelease `values.…image.repository` sites keep the
  // harbor packageName and keep the old behaviour. Any packageRule keying on one of these must
  // therefore list BOTH forms; see the sparkyfitness rule.
  registryAliases: {
    "harbor.webgrip.dev/dockerhub": "docker.io",
  },
  // Schedule: the shared preset ships "after 3am and before 7am every weekday" + "every weekend"
  // (Europe/Amsterdam). That window was already MOOT for almost everything here — the digest,
  // patch, minor, vulnerability, OCIRepository-patch and dev/staging/production rules below each
  // override schedule to "at any time", and PR #504 (a digest bump) was demonstrably created at
  // 09:15 Amsterdam, well outside it. What the window still bound was the handful of rules with
  // no override: mise tools, GitHub Actions, the Flux controllers group and lock file
  // maintenance. Those fared badly. The window opens 03:00 Amsterdam (01:00 UTC) and the only
  // job run overlapping it ended ~01:10-01:20 UTC, leaving those managers roughly 7-17 MINUTES
  // of eligibility per weekday — which is why mise and Actions PRs crawled while container
  // bumps flowed. Weekends were unrestricted, so the behaviour looked random.
  // Setting it here makes the cadence uniform and honest: the soak (minimumReleaseAge) is the
  // deliberate safety gate, not a clock window that silently applies to four managers.
  schedule: [
    "at any time"
  ],
  // Concurrency: the shared preset ships prConcurrentLimit 5 and Renovate takes the LOWEST
  // value among a branch's upgrades, so the admin ConfigMap's 10 never applied — 5 open PRs
  // (three of them hand-edited and unmergeable) deadlocked PR creation entirely and every
  // update rendered as "Rate-Limited". 15 restores headroom while keeping CI bursts survivable
  // on the single worker node (each PR triggers an e2e run, and flux-local is disk-IOPS bound).
  // branchConcurrentLimit must match or branches cap before PRs do.
  prConcurrentLimit: 15,
  branchConcurrentLimit: 15,
  // The preset ships rebaseWhen "behind-base-branch", which throttles automerge to roughly ONE
  // PR per run here: merging the first PR moves main, every other renovate branch is then
  // "behind", each gets rebased, the rebase restarts its four e2e checks, and none can merge
  // until the next run 6h later. `main` also has concurrent human/agent writers, so branches
  // fall behind constantly even when Renovate merges nothing — every rebase re-runs CI on the
  // single worker node. "conflicted" rebases only on a real conflict, so a run can merge many
  // PRs.
  //
  // THE TRADE-OFF (corrected 2026-08-04 — the previous note here claimed "branch protection sets
  // no required checks and Renovate does not wait for e2e", which is false and cost us a week of
  // frozen PRs). Forgejo Actions posts a commit status per e2e job, so every PR carries four
  // `e2e / …` statuses and Renovate's automerge DOES gate on them. Combined with "conflicted",
  // that means: a branch's head SHA never moves while main advances, so CI never re-runs, so a
  // branch that went red once STAYS RED FOREVER and can never automerge — even after main is
  // fixed. On 2026-08-04 nine PRs were wedged this way on statuses dated Jul 29–Aug 1, left over
  // from a CI outage that main had already fixed.
  //
  // We keep "conflicted" anyway: the rebase storm it prevents is real and the single worker node
  // cannot absorb it. The price is that clearing a red is a MANUAL step — tick the PR's rebase
  // checkbox on the Renovate Dashboard 🤖 issue to rebuild it against current main. If PRs stop
  // merging, check for stale statuses before suspecting Renovate itself.
  rebaseWhen: "conflicted",
  // Per-PR escape hatch for the trade-off above. `conflicted` means a branch's head never moves
  // while main advances, so a PR that went red once can never re-test itself. Adding this label
  // to a single PR tells Renovate to keep that one branch rebased on main — targeted, without
  // reintroducing the repo-wide rebase storm a single worker node cannot absorb. Supported on the
  // forgejo platform. PREREQUISITE: the label must exist in the Forgejo repo before it can be
  // applied; Renovate consumes it, it does not create it.
  keepUpdatedLabel: "renovate/keep-updated",
  // NOTE: abandonmentThreshold is deliberately NOT set here. It looked missing, but the shared
  // preset extends `abandonments:recommended`, which already applies a 1-year threshold to
  // matchPackageNames ["*"] AND ships curated per-package exceptions (packages upstream considers
  // finished are marked "eternal" so they are never falsely flagged). Setting it here would be
  // redundant at best and would flatten those curated exceptions at worst.
  // NOTE on Harbor-proxied charts: non-bootstrap OCI Helm charts have their
  // OCIRepository url pointed at Harbor's pull-through proxy (harbor.${SECRET_DOMAIN}/ghcr/…,
  // ADR-0023/0017). Renovate does its version lookup *through* Harbor — verified that
  // Harbor 2.15's proxy returns the full upstream tag list (not just cached tags), so
  // discovery is complete. CORRECTED 2026-08-05: this used to claim "registryAliases is NOT
  // used: its keys match on registry host only, so they can't disambiguate multiple upstreams
  // behind one Harbor host+path." That is false — Renovate matches a PATH PREFIX
  // (`currentFrom.startsWith(name + "/")`), so `harbor.webgrip.dev/dockerhub` disambiguates
  // perfectly. It IS now used for the dockerhub project; see registryAliases at the top. The
  // ghcr/quay projects stay unaliased on purpose (no upstream timestamps to gain).
  // Pinned @sha256: digests stay valid (proxy is content-addressable). The two packageRules
  // below that keyed on ghcr.io are widened to also match the harbor.…/ghcr proxy path.
  ignorePaths: [
    "**/*.sops.*",
    "age.key",
    "cloudflare-tunnel.json",
    "github-deploy.key",
    "github-deploy.key.pub",
    "github-push-token.txt",
    "kubeconfig",
    // The app is not referenced from observability/kustomization.yaml.
    "kubernetes/apps/observability/twitch-exporter/**",
    // Kyverno CLI tests intentionally use fake registries/images to exercise policy failures.
    "kubernetes/apps/kyverno/tests/**",
    // Kyverno policies use wildcard image patterns (*, !*, gcr.io/*) that Renovate misparses as Docker deps.
    "kubernetes/apps/kyverno/policies/**",
  ],
  postUpgradeTasks: {
    commands: [
      "./scripts/update-oci-digests.sh"
    ],
    fileFilters: [
      "kubernetes/apps/**/ocirepository.yaml"
    ],
    executionMode: "branch",
  },
  // The Flux manager's DEFAULT pattern only matches `flux-system/<file>.yaml` (one path
  // segment), which in this tree matches nearly nothing — so every classic (non-OCI)
  // HelmRepository chart (trivy-operator, longhorn, harbor, authentik, keda, falco, the
  // observability stack, … ~17 apps) never got a version PR at all. Found 2026-08-29:
  // trivy-operator sat at chart 0.32.1 with 0.36.0 four weeks released and no branch.
  // Widening is safe for the OCI estate: OCIRepository files are owned by custom.regex +
  // update-oci-digests.sh, and the flux manager is disabled on them by packageRule below.
  flux: {
    managerFilePatterns: [
      "/(^|/)kubernetes/apps/.+\\.ya?ml$/",
    ],
  },
  // PR body columns (Released / "Age (d)", self-hosted from releaseTimestamp /
  // newVersionAgeInDays) moved to the shared preset in v1.6.0 — they apply org-wide, not just
  // here. The preset also carries the generic notes (major/security/grouped/soak explainer), so
  // this repo no longer re-states them: prBodyNotes is MERGEABLE, and locally-restated variants
  // render as near-duplicate lines in every PR body. Only the note below stays, because it is
  // homelab POLICY the generic preset text cannot know.
  prBodyNotes: [
    "Homelab soak tiers: patch 1d, minor 3d, digest/vulnerability 0 days; platform-critical minors 7d + dashboard approval; talos/** always manual. A green PR merges on the first hourly Renovate run after its soak expires.",
  ],
  customManagers: [
    {
      customType: "regex",
      description: "The CNPG operator's default Postgres image (every Cluster without imageName runs it) is a bare image string in the chart's config.data, which no built-in manager reads.",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/(^|/)kubernetes/apps/cnpg-system/cloudnative-pg/app/helmrelease\\.ya?ml$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "POSTGRES_IMAGE_NAME:\\s*[\"']?(?<depName>[^:\"'\\s]+):(?<currentValue>[^@\"'\\s]+)@(?<currentDigest>sha256:[a-f0-9]{64})",
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: "docker",
    },
  ],
  packageRules: [
    {
      // Digest-only updates change WHICH bytes a tag resolves to, not the version;
      // upstream tag re-pushes (e.g. coredns chart 1.46.0, three digests so far)
      // otherwise wedge verify-oci-digests CI until a human approves the fix.
      description: "Digest-only updates skip dashboard approval and automerge once checks pass",
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "digest"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: false,
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "pr",
      minimumReleaseAge: "0 days",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
    },
    {
      // AUTOMERGE POLICY (2026-07): patch+minor+digest ride unattended; majors stay behind the
      // preset's dashboard-approval gate. The shared preset ZEROES minimumReleaseAge for
      // approval-gated types ("approval IS the gate"), so dropping the approval gate here
      // REQUIRES re-asserting the soak explicitly. The soak is the only time gate: Forgejo
      // branch protection requires no status checks and the commit-status API is empty, so
      // Renovate's PR-automerge does NOT wait for e2e. These two rules sit BEFORE the vuln
      // rules (which keep their 0-day fast path), the cluster-critical minor gate, and the
      // talos gate — all of which come later in this array and therefore win field-by-field.
      description: "Blanket automerge: patch updates go unattended after a 1-day soak",
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: false,
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "pr",
      minimumReleaseAge: "1 day",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "Blanket automerge: minor updates go unattended after a 3-day soak",
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "minor"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: false,
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "pr",
      minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "Prioritize vulnerability fixes ahead of all routine dependency updates; 0-day soak keeps the security fast path ahead of the blanket patch/minor soaks above",
      matchJsonata: [
        "$exists(vulnerabilityFixVersion)"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: "0 days",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
      prPriority: 20,
    },
    {
      description: "Automerge patch-level vulnerability fixes after checks pass",
      matchJsonata: [
        "$exists(vulnerabilityFixVersion) and updateType = 'patch'"
      ],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "branch",
      dependencyDashboardApproval: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: "0 days",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
      prPriority: 20,
    },
    {
      description: "Prioritize updates for cluster-critical platform paths ahead of normal app updates",
      matchFileNames: [
        "bootstrap/**",
        "talos/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/cert-manager/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/cnpg-dr/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/cnpg-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/flux-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/kube-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/longhorn-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/network/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/renovate/**",
      ],
      prPriority: 10,
    },
    {
      description: "Group Flux controller updates and keep Renovate's Flux manager away from digest-pinned OCIRepository files. OCIRepository tags are updated by custom.regex and scripts/update-oci-digests.sh refreshes spec.ref.digest from the registry manifest digest. Scoped to flux-system since the manager pattern widening (2026-08-29) — classic app charts must NOT collapse into this group.",
      matchManagers: [
        "flux"
      ],
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/flux-system/**"
      ],
      groupName: "Flux controllers",
      pinDigests: false,
      separateMinorPatch: true,
      separateMultipleMajor: true,
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "The widened Flux manager must not double-manage OCIRepository files: custom.regex owns their tag bumps and update-oci-digests.sh their spec.ref.digest — a second manager on the same field races the digest refresh.",
      matchManagers: [
        "flux"
      ],
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/**/ocirepository.yaml"
      ],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Platform-critical classic Helm charts (storage, registry, database operators, the secrets backend) never automerge — same policy as the OCI platform group, for the charts that only exist as classic HelmRepository.",
      matchManagers: [
        "flux"
      ],
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/longhorn-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/harbor/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/cnpg-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/security/openbao/**"
      ],
      automerge: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Omnigraph is pre-1.0 and changes its storage format on minor releases; an upgrade needs a quiesced `omnigraph upgrade` against the PVC before the new image can open the graph, so its bumps are never unattended.",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/ai/omnigraph/**"
      ],
      automerge: false,
    },
    {
      description: "The omnigraph CLI and server upgrade together (a CLI newer than the server's storage format cannot drive it), so the mise-pinned CLI is never bumped unattended either.",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/omnigraph/i"
      ],
      automerge: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Automerge Flux OCI Helm chart patch updates; digest pins are refreshed by post-upgrade tasks",
      matchManagers: [
        "custom.regex"
      ],
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/**/ocirepository.yaml"
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch"
      ],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "pr",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
    },
    {
      // Splits the shared preset's single "Flux OCI Helm charts" group in two. That preset rule
      // exists to keep pinDigests off (spec.ref.digest is a separate YAML key); its groupName
      // rode along and was never a considered decision, and the result was PR #449: FIFTEEN
      // unrelated charts in one commit — cert-manager, coredns, kyverno and envoy-gateway
      // alongside pyroscope and opencost — automerging unattended via the rule directly above,
      // because the cluster-critical gate below only matches `minor`.
      //
      // Three concrete problems with that, in order of how much they cost:
      //  1. Rollback is all-or-nothing. If coredns breaks DNS, reverting takes cert-manager and
      //     thirteen others with it. There is no partial revert of a grouped bump.
      //  2. Group branches fail ATOMICALLY — one dep Renovate cannot resolve aborts the whole
      //     branch ("Error updating branch: update failure"), so a single bad chart blocks the
      //     other fourteen indefinitely. #448/#449 sat open from 2026-07-31 for exactly this shape.
      //  3. A post-merge cluster fault has fifteen candidate causes in one commit.
      //
      // Grouping is still RIGHT for the non-critical remainder: ungrouping entirely would mean
      // ~60 e2e jobs on the single disk-IOPS-bound worker and, at 1-2 merges per Renovate run,
      // most of a day to drain. That is the pressure that timed out three PRs on 2026-08-04.
      // So this keeps the CI saving everywhere and only removes UNATTENDED merging from the
      // platform paths — they still group, still get one PR, but a human presses merge.
      //
      // Path list mirrors the cluster-critical gate below, plus TWO additions that list omits.
      // Both are flagged rather than assumed — if either omission was deliberate, drop it here
      // and the rest of the rule still holds:
      //   kyverno  — admission control; a broken webhook fails every create in the cluster,
      //              which is at least as load-bearing as cert-manager.
      //   forgejo  — the git source Flux reconciles FROM. Break it and GitOps stops delivering,
      //              including the fix; you are then repairing the delivery system by hand.
      //              Running workloads survive, so this is recoverable rather than fatal, but it
      //              is the one component whose failure removes your ability to ship a revert.
      description: "Platform-critical Flux OCI Helm charts group separately and never automerge — a 15-chart bundle spanning cert-manager/coredns/kyverno/envoy-gateway cannot be partially reverted",
      matchManagers: [
        "custom.regex"
      ],
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/cert-manager/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/cnpg-dr/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/cnpg-system/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/flux-system/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/forgejo/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/harbor/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/kube-system/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/kyverno/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/longhorn-system/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/network/**/ocirepository.yaml",
        "kubernetes/apps/renovate/**/ocirepository.yaml",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch"
      ],
      groupName: "Flux OCI Helm charts (platform)",
      additionalBranchPrefix: "platform-",
      automerge: false,
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Disable digest pinning for talos/ version files — talhelper uses plain semver strings; appending @sha256: makes talenv.yaml unparseable",
      matchFileNames: [
        "talos/**"
      ],
      pinDigests: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Flux Operator Group",
      groupName: "flux-operator",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/flux-operator/",
        "/flux-instance/",
        "/flux-operator-manifests/"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
      commitBodyTable: true,
      minimumGroupSize: 1,
    },
    {
      description: "Use fully qualified Postgres Alpine tags",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "postgres",
        "docker.io/library/postgres",
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/dockerhub/library/postgres"
      ],
      allowedVersions: "/^\\d+\\.\\d+-alpine\\d+\\.\\d+$/",
    },
    {
      description: "Group GitHub Actions updates separately from GitOps/runtime dependency updates",
      matchManagers: [
        "github-actions"
      ],
      groupName: "GitHub Actions",
      automerge: false,
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Auto-merge GitHub Actions patch/digest/minor updates; minor is included here because the group rule above sets automerge:false after the blanket rules and would otherwise re-block actions minors",
      matchManagers: [
        "github-actions"
      ],
      pinDigests: true,
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "branch",
      minimumReleaseAge: "1 day",
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch",
        "digest",
        "minor"
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "Group Mise tool updates separately from GitOps/runtime dependency updates",
      matchManagers: [
        "mise"
      ],
      groupName: "Mise tools",
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Auto-merge Mise tool patch updates only after checks pass",
      matchManagers: [
        "mise"
      ],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "branch",
      minimumReleaseAge: "1 day",
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch"
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "Hold Python at the last installable mise pin; python@3.14.4 and python@3.14.5 currently fail mise install because the upstream standalone artifact is missing a lib directory.",
      matchManagers: [
        "mise"
      ],
      matchDepNames: [
        "python"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "python/cpython"
      ],
      allowedVersions: "<=3.14.2",
    },
    {
      // Kyverno chart 3.10 ships Kyverno 1.20, which REMOVES the legacy kyverno.io/v1
      // ClusterPolicy API. 22 of those are still live in kubernetes/apps/kyverno/policies/app/
      // (83 rules) pending the CEL migration; taking 1.20 before that finishes deletes the
      // policy estate, silently and all at once.
      //
      // The chart->app mapping is exact and verified against the registry, not assumed:
      //   3.5.1 -> v1.15.1   3.6.0 -> v1.16.0   3.7.0 -> v1.17.0   3.8.0 -> v1.18.0
      // so 3.9 -> 1.19 (legacy deprecated but PRESENT, fine) and 3.10 -> 1.20 (removed).
      // <3.10.0 therefore still allows 3.8.x patches and the whole 3.9 line.
      //
      // This is a seatbelt, not a fire: kyverno is already in the cluster-critical gate
      // below, so a minor needs dashboard approval and never automerges. What this stops
      // is someone APPROVING that minor without knowing what 1.20 removes.
      //
      // REMOVE THIS RULE once `kubectl get clusterpolicy` returns zero
      // (rfc-kyverno-cel-migration.md).
      // The `bao` CLI is pinned to the CLUSTER'S SERVER version on purpose (.mise.toml:
      // chart 0.28.3 / appVersion v2.5.4) so client and KV-v2 behaviour match. Renovate
      // cannot know that, so an automatic bump is always wrong here — the correct trigger
      // is upgrading the OpenBao chart, not a new CLI release.
      //
      // It is also the dependency that proved how expensive getting this wrong is. The
      // 2026-08-10 bump to 2.6.1 broke the entire macOS arm64 toolchain: upstream renamed
      // their release assets (bao_* -> openbao_*), the aqua package still expects the old
      // name, and `mise install` is all-or-nothing — so one unresolvable tool took kubectl,
      // flux, helm and the pre-commit hooks down with it. The guard-secrets hook then
      // reported a phantom "plaintext secret" because its gitleaks fallback runs through
      // `mise exec`, which was itself broken.
      //
      // dependencyDashboardApproval rather than a hard ignore: still surface new releases
      // so the pin can be moved deliberately when the server is upgraded.
      description: "Never bump the OpenBao CLI unattended — it is pinned to the cluster's server version, and 2.6.1 broke the whole macOS toolchain via an upstream asset rename",
      // matchDepNames ONLY. Renovate ANDs the match* fields, and the first version of
      // this rule also carried matchPackageNames: ["openbao/openbao"] — which is not the
      // package name Renovate resolves for this dep, so the whole rule never matched and
      // ca86264a re-bumped it to the broken 2.6.1 the very next run. Do not add
      // matchPackageNames back without confirming the resolved name in a Renovate log.
      matchManagers: [
        "mise"
      ],
      matchDepNames: [
        "aqua:openbao/openbao/bao"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: true,
      automerge: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Hold the Kyverno chart below 3.10 until the CEL migration completes — 3.10 ships Kyverno 1.20, which removes the legacy kyverno.io/v1 ClusterPolicy API that 22 live policies still use",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/kyverno/charts/kyverno"
      ],
      allowedVersions: "<3.10.0",
    },
    {
      description: "Hold the Authentik OpenTofu provider on the server's minor — 2026.8.0 against the 2026.5 chart failed every provider read on a field the older API does not return; bump this ceiling in the same PR as the authentik chart",
      matchDatasources: [
        "terraform-provider"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "goauthentik/authentik"
      ],
      allowedVersions: "<2026.6.0",
    },
    {
      description: "Group application container image references outside the Kubernetes GitOps tree",
      matchManagers: [
        "dockerfile",
        "docker-compose"
      ],
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      groupName: "application container images",
      pinDigests: true,
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Gate minor updates for cluster-critical platform files. automerge:false is load-bearing: a group branch (harbor suite, shared app-template) automerges only when EVERY member has automerge:true, so this keeps an approved critical minor from riding an otherwise-automerging group to main unattended.",
      matchFileNames: [
        "bootstrap/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/cert-manager/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/cnpg-dr/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/cnpg-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/flux-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/harbor/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/kube-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/longhorn-system/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/network/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/renovate/**",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "minor"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: true,
      automerge: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: "7 days",
    },
    {
      description: "Talos and kubelet stay fully manual (patch AND minor): merging talenv.yaml deploys nothing — task talos:* applies it later — so an unattended bump silently changes what the next manual upgrade rolls out. Approval alone is the gate; 0-day soak avoids the discarded-approval-tick trap (a tick during a soak window is consumed and dropped).",
      matchFileNames: [
        "talos/**"
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch",
        "minor"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: true,
      automerge: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: "0 days",
    },
    {
      description: "Treat missing release timestamps as stable for GHCR/quay Docker datasource updates; Renovate only supports Docker release timestamps on Docker Hub, so GHCR OCI artifacts can otherwise stay pending forever. Also covers charts proxied through Harbor (harbor.${SECRET_DOMAIN}/<project>/…), whose timestamps are likewise unavailable. quay.io was added 2026-08-04: without it `renovate/stability-days` sits pending FOREVER on every quay dep, which does not merely delay the soak — it holds the branch status non-green, so automerge never fires and the PR is stuck for good (cert-manager #467 sat 6 days on a 1-day soak). Registries land here because they cannot report a timestamp, not because we want to skip the soak.",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/^ghcr\\.io\\//",
        "/^quay\\.io\\//",
        "/^harbor\\.webgrip\\.dev\\//"
      ],
      minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour: "timestamp-optional",
    },
    {
      description: "Ungroup Kubernetes app container images; overrides the global 'GitOps container images' group so each dependency uses Renovate's normal per-dependency branch/topic. The shared app-template and shared busybox rules below still win for those packages because they come last.",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/**",
        "!kubernetes/apps/**/ocirepository.yaml"
      ],
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      groupName: null,
    },
    {
      description: "Group the goharbor suite (Helm chart + the lockstep component images pinned in the HelmRelease) into one PR. Upstream releases them together (chart 1.19.x ↔ app v2.15.x); per-image PRs merge partially and leave Harbor running mixed component versions. Must stay AFTER the ungroup rule above so it wins.",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker",
        "helm"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/^docker\\.io\\/goharbor\\//",
        "/^harbor\\.webgrip\\.dev\\/dockerhub\\/goharbor\\//",
        "harbor", // the goharbor Helm chart (helm.goharbor.io)
      ],
      groupName: "harbor suite",
      additionalBranchPrefix: "harbor-suite-",
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Group the KEDA suite (Helm chart + the three kedacore images digest-pinned in the HelmRelease) into one PR. The chart passes operator flags that only the matching image accepts: chart 2.21.0 against image 2.20.2 crashlooped on 'unknown flag: --service-account-token-mode' (2026-09-26), as chart 2.20.2 against image 2.20.0 did on --http-max-idle-conns (2026-08-29). Both times the chart merged alone, the upgrade timed out, rolled back, and stalled every forgejo runner Kustomization behind KEDA. Must stay AFTER the ungroup rule above so it wins.",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker",
        "helm"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/^ghcr\\.io\\/kedacore\\//",
        "/^harbor\\.webgrip\\.dev\\/ghcr\\/kedacore\\//",
        "keda",
      ],
      groupName: "keda suite",
      additionalBranchPrefix: "keda-suite-",
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Group shared app-template updates into a dedicated PR (matches both the upstream ref and the Harbor pull-through proxy path).",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/bjw-s-labs/helm/app-template",
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/ghcr/bjw-s-labs/helm/app-template"
      ],
      groupName: "shared app-template",
      additionalBranchPrefix: "shared-app-template-",
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Group shared busybox updates into a dedicated PR",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "docker.io/library/busybox",
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/dockerhub/library/busybox"
      ],
      groupName: "shared busybox",
      additionalBranchPrefix: "shared-busybox-",
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      // Upstream tags this project with FOUR numeric components (v0.15.5.4, v0.16.6.3) alongside
      // 2- and 3-component ones. The default docker versioning can't parse a 4th segment, so
      // Renovate couldn't anchor the pinned value and reported "Can't find version matching
      // v0.16.6.3" — it offered no updates at all while upstream moved to v0.17.x. `loose`
      // compares arbitrary dot-separated numerics and ignores non-version tags (dev, latest).
      // Grouped because the web and server images ship in lockstep and MUST move together:
      // separate PRs merge independently and would leave the UI and API on different versions
      // (the same failure that left Harbor on chart 1.19.1 with v2.15.2 component images).
      description: "sparkyfitness: loose versioning for 4-component tags, and keep web+server in lockstep",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      // BOTH forms are load-bearing. registryAliases rewrites packageName to docker.io/... for
      // managers it supports, but NOT for helm-values — and these two images are declared in a
      // HelmRelease values block. List only one form and this rule silently stops matching for
      // half the tree, which costs `versioning: loose` and brings back the exact
      // "Can't find version matching v0.16.6.3" wedge documented below.
      matchPackageNames: [
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/dockerhub/codewithcj/sparkyfitness",
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/dockerhub/codewithcj/sparkyfitness_server",
        "docker.io/codewithcj/sparkyfitness",
        "docker.io/codewithcj/sparkyfitness_server"
      ],
      versioning: "loose",
      groupName: "sparkyfitness",
      additionalBranchPrefix: "sparkyfitness-",
      commitBodyTable: true,
    },
    {
      // Environment promotion policy (erfbeeld first; any app adopting the
      // dev/staging/production tree inherits it). Branch model: the app repo's `development`
      // branch cuts prerelease versions (<v>-development.N — the staging channel) and `main`
      // cuts stables (the production channel). dev + staging follow prereleases AND stables
      // automatically (ignoreUnstable: false + automerge); production follows stables only,
      // also automatically — the human gate is merging development -> main in the app repo,
      // not merging a Renovate PR here. Digest-only refreshes are excluded here on purpose —
      // they re-resolve the same tag, not a new version, and keep automerging everywhere via
      // the digest rule above.
      // Renovate forbids combining matchUpdateTypes with version-selection
      // options like ignoreUnstable in one rule, so each env policy is a
      // pair: channel rule (path -> ignoreUnstable) + automerge rule
      // (path + update types -> automerge).
      description: "Pre-production envs (dev/staging) track the prerelease channel",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/*/dev/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/*/staging/**",
      ],
      ignoreUnstable: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Pre-production envs (dev/staging): all chart+image tag bumps automerge, prereleases included",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/*/dev/**",
        "kubernetes/apps/*/staging/**",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch",
        "minor",
        "major"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: false,
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "pr",
      minimumReleaseAge: "0 days",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "Production env sees stable releases only — prereleases never reach it",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/*/production/**"
      ],
      ignoreUnstable: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Production env tracks stable releases automatically; promotion happens by merging development -> main in the app repo",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/*/production/**"
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "patch",
        "minor",
        "major"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: false,
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "pr",
      minimumReleaseAge: "0 days",
      schedule: [
        "at any time"
      ],
    },
    {
      // Ploeg is the one app here that has never cut a stable release: it ships
      // 0.2.0-rc.N only. The env-promotion rules above buy prerelease tracking
      // with a PATH (kubernetes/apps/*/dev|staging/**), and ploeg does not use
      // that tree, so it fell through to the default stability filter — which
      // declines to offer an -rc version and says nothing about it. The chart
      // was detected the whole time; its dependency-dashboard entry simply
      // carried no "Updates" annotation, unlike every stable-versioned chart
      // beside it. The pin therefore sat on rc.17 while rc.18-rc.27 published
      // green, and only a hand-written bump ever moved it.
      //
      // Verified NOT to be the cause before landing this, since each looked
      // likelier: Renovate does bump OCIRepository charts here (three Renovate
      // Bot commits), it does hold Harbor robot credentials for the PRIVATE
      // webgrip project (RENOVATE_HOST_RULES + HARBOR_ROBOT_*), and it does
      // track another chart from that same project (charts/erfbeeld). The one
      // property ploeg does not share with any of them is the prerelease
      // version, and no prerelease dep anywhere in this repo is offered an
      // update.
      //
      // No automerge rule pairs with this one, deliberately. The pin's own
      // comment records that tag and values move together, and every bump so
      // far has reasoned about values compatibility by hand; a PR keeps that
      // gate, where automerging a first-party chart would remove it. (Renovate
      // forbids combining matchUpdateTypes with ignoreUnstable anyway, so an
      // automerge policy would have to be a second rule — there is none.)
      description: "Ploeg and Vloer charts remain on reviewed 0.x prereleases",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/ploeg/**/ocirepository.yaml"
      ],
      allowedVersions: "/^0\\./",
      automerge: false,
      ignoreUnstable: false,
    },
    {
      // The SECOND knob, and the reason the rule above is not enough on its own.
      // ploeg pins its chart in ocirepository.yaml and its image in
      // helmrelease.yaml, and the two must move together or the Helm upgrade
      // reports success against a binary that never changed. The rule above
      // covers only the chart file, so the image fell through to the default
      // stability filter — which declines to offer an -rc version and says
      // nothing about it. Left as it was, the next Renovate run would bump the
      // chart to the newest rc and strand the image, which is precisely the
      // rc.27 split that motivated annotating this line in the first place
      // (2026-08-26: chart moved, binary stayed on rc.17, pod never rolled).
      // The pin sat on rc.28 while rc.29-rc.31 published green.
      //
      // Scoped by depName, NOT by widening the rule above to the whole file:
      // helmrelease.yaml also pins agent-runner and docker-dind, and those track
      // stable channels. A file-level ignoreUnstable would start offering THEM
      // prereleases — a docker:*-rc on the dind sidecar of every agent run.
      //
      // No automerge here either, for the same reason recorded above: tag and
      // values move together and every bump so far has reasoned about values
      // compatibility by hand. A PR keeps that gate.
      description: "Ploeg and Vloer images remain on reviewed 0.x prereleases",
      matchFileNames: [
        "kubernetes/apps/ploeg/**/helmrelease.yaml"
      ],
      matchDepNames: [
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/webgrip/ploegd",
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/webgrip/de-vloer",
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/webgrip/de-vloer-agent"
      ],
      allowedVersions: "/^0\\./",
      automerge: false,
      ignoreUnstable: false,
    },
    {
      description: "Group Grafana.com dashboard revision updates into one reviewable PR",
      matchDatasources: [
        "custom.grafana-dashboards"
      ],
      groupName: "Grafana dashboards",
      additionalBranchPrefix: "grafana-dashboards-",
      commitBodyTable: true,
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/grafana-dashboard"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      semanticCommitScope: "container",
      commitMessageTopic: "image {{depName}}",
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/container"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "helm"
      ],
      semanticCommitScope: "helm",
      commitMessageTopic: "chart {{depName}}",
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/helm"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchManagers: [
        "github-actions"
      ],
      semanticCommitType: "ci",
      semanticCommitScope: "github-action",
      commitMessageTopic: "action {{depName}}",
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/github-action"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchDatasources: [
        "github-releases"
      ],
      semanticCommitScope: "github-release",
      commitMessageTopic: "release {{depName}}",
      addLabels: [
        "renovate/github-release"
      ],
    },
    {
      matchManagers: [
        "mise"
      ],
      semanticCommitScope: "mise",
      commitMessageTopic: "tool {{depName}}",
    },
    {
      description: "searxng: re-attach the shared calver+sha versioning to the Harbor-proxied path. The gitops preset carries home-operations' versioning/searxng rule, but it keys on packageName ghcr.io/searxng/searxng, and this image is declared at a helm-values site (HelmRelease values.…image.repository) which registryAliases does NOT rewrite — so after the image moved behind harbor.webgrip.dev/ghcr the preset stopped matching. With the default docker versioning unable to parse the '<calver>-<commit sha>' tag shape, Renovate offered nothing at all and the image silently froze on 2025.12.9 for nine months while upstream shipped the engine-scraper fixes that keep results working. Same BOTH-FORMS failure mode as the sparkyfitness rule above.",
      matchDatasources: [
        "docker"
      ],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/searxng/searxng",
        "harbor.webgrip.dev/ghcr/searxng/searxng"
      ],
      versioning: "regex:^(?<major>\\d+)\\.(?<minor>\\d+)\\.(?<patch>\\d+)-[a-f0-9]+$",
    },
    {
      description: "CNPG barman-cloud plugin: the chart (controller) and its sidecar image are pinned separately (the sidecar by digest, so CNPG pods pass image-supply-chain-digest-pinned without an exception) but must move together — a sidecar ahead of or behind the controller speaks the wrong CNPG-I contract to every database pod.",
      groupName: "cnpg-plugin-barman-cloud",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "plugin-barman-cloud",
        "ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/plugin-barman-cloud-sidecar"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
      commitBodyTable: true,
      minimumGroupSize: 1,
    },
    {
      description: "CNPG operator: the chart (CRDs, RBAC) and the digest-pinned operator image, which also runs as every database pod's bootstrap-controller, are pinned separately but must move together — an image ahead of or behind the chart's CRDs is a broken operator.",
      groupName: "cnpg-operator",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "cloudnative-pg",
        "ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/cloudnative-pg"
      ],
      group: {
        commitMessageTopic: "{{{groupName}}} group",
      },
      commitBodyTable: true,
      minimumGroupSize: 1,
    },
    {
      description: "The operator's default Postgres image is every CNPG database at once: a major bump rolls all of them onto a new Postgres major. Offer it, never merge it without a human.",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgresql"
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: [
        "major"
      ],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: true,
      automerge: false,
    },
  ],
}